A||N||5||nette||"a running fiero won't shift"||||8||
Z||000000||nette||04-24-2001||09:19 PM||||Help!  My search through the archives didn't produce the results I'd hoped for.<P>My 87 4cyl 5spd coupe rather suddenly stopped shifting the other day.  I had to ram it into gear to get it back home.  Now it won't go into any gear while running.<P>It started out just getting harder to shift into 1st and sometimes 2nd.  We checked the fluid and it was low.  Added fluid.  No change.  Bled system.  Better resistance in pedal but still no-go on the shift-o.<P>There doesn't appear to be any leaking around slave or master cylinders.<P>Is my baby in mechanical trouble??????<P>------------------<BR>nette<BR>87 coupe||||reg||8
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||04-24-2001||09:26 PM||||Hey Nette!<BR>Get a 6 inch rule and measure the travel of the slave push rod...<BR>It needs 1.1 inch minimum with 1.2 inch maximum available...<BR>If you do not get over one inch travel, check the bleeding again and look for fluid inside the passenger compartment under the master cylinder...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||nette||04-24-2001||09:32 PM||||Hey Crash!  Thanks for the info.  I will measure the travel as soon as my hubby gets home.<P>As far as fluid leaking in the cab I already checked and there is no obvious leak.  I got down in there and looked with a flashlight and it looks clean.||||reg||1
Z||000003||strdream||04-24-2001||09:44 PM||||For some reason the clutch isn't releasing completely. If you are getting the full travel of the arm than you are going to have to go inside and fix it.  It could be the disc going to pieces or something broken on the pressure plate.<BR>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000004||nette||04-24-2001||09:48 PM||||Thanks Ross *I think*  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>That's what I'm afraid of, mechanical problems.  So if it's clutch work that needs to be done my hubby thinks we would have to drop the cradle and everything.  Is this true or can it be done right in the car?<P>I'm gonna go thumb through my fs manual again...that thing is getting a lot of use lately!||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||04-26-2001||07:00 PM||||Based on what I've read here, in order to service the clutch internals, you must drop, or at least rock, the cradle.  ||||reg||1
